.TH GGE\-DIALOG 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-dialog \- typewriter speech boxes and conversations
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \fBDialog\fR (dialog.h) shows one line of speech in a box across the bottom of the screen: an optional speaker name in yellow, the text revealed character by character, word\-wrapped to three lines. The player presses SPACE, ENTER, PAD_SOUTH or clicks to complete the line, then again to dismiss it. One Dialog shows one line at a time; a conversation is a sequence of lines driven by dialog_done(3). It is presentation state (it keeps pointers to the strings), so it lives in a scene static.
.PP
.SH ONE LINE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include "dialog.h"
static Dialog dlg;                                  /* zero: debug font, 40 chars/s */
\&
static void on_enter(Engine *e, Scene *s) { dlg = (Dialog){ .font = font, .chars_per_sec = 50 }; }
\&
/* somewhere in update, when the player talks to a sign */
dialog_show(&dlg, NULL, "The cat cafe is closed on Mondays.");
\&
static void handle_input(Engine *e, Scene *s, const Input *in) {
  if (!dialog_done(&dlg)) { dialog_update(&dlg, in, e\->dt); return; }   /* the box eats input */
  ... normal input ...
}
static void render(Engine *e, Scene *s, float alpha) {
  ... world, HUD ...
  dialog_render(&dlg, e);                            /* last, so it is on top */
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
While a line is showing, the scene decides what else happens: the example above swallows all input, and update can check \fBdialog_done\fR to freeze the player while keeping enemies moving, or freeze everything, as the game wants. dialog_render draws nothing when inactive, so it can always be called.
.PP
.SH CONVERSATIONS
Lines are data; the scene walks them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
typedef struct Line { const char *who, *text; } Line;
static const Line shopkeeper[] = {
  { "Shopkeeper", "Welcome. Fish is on sale." },
  { "You",        "Do you take yarn?" },
  { "Shopkeeper", "Only the good kind." },
};
static const Line *convo; static int convo_len, convo_at;
\&
static void start_convo(const Line *lines, int n) { convo = lines; convo_len = n; convo_at = 0; dialog_show(&dlg, lines[0].who, lines[0].text); }
\&
static void update(Engine *e, Scene *s, float dt) {
  if (convo && dialog_done(&dlg)) {
    if (++convo_at < convo_len) dialog_show(&dlg, convo[convo_at].who, convo[convo_at].text);
    else { convo = NULL; on_convo_end(e); }        /* give the item, set the flag */
  }
  ...
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Branching: a conversation's last line can open a two\-item menu scene (gge\-menu(7)) whose actions start the next conversation. Conditions: pick the array from GameState flags before starting (\fBg\->flags[MET_SHOPKEEPER] ? repeat_lines : first_lines\fR). For cutscene\-style delivery with movement and fades between lines, use gge\-cutscene(7), whose CUT_SAY steps use this module.
.PP
.SH TEXT FROM GAMESTATE
The Dialog keeps the string pointer, not a copy. Literals and static tables are safe; a line built with snprintf must go into a static buffer that stays untouched until the line is dismissed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static char line[128];
snprintf(line, sizeof line, "You found %d coins!", n);
dialog_show(&dlg, NULL, line);
.EE
.RE
.PP
.SH APPEARANCE
The box is drawn from the engine's defaults: translucent black with a white outline, 8 px inset from the screen edges, tall enough for the speaker plus three lines of the font. Change the font and speed through the struct; for a differently styled box (portrait, custom frame), draw your own panel and portrait in render \fIbefore\fR dialog_render, sized to match, or reimplement the few lines of dialog_render with text_draw (gge\-text(7)) and keep dialog_update for the typing and input logic.
.PP
.SH SPEED AND SKIPPING
\fBchars_per_sec\fR (0 = 40) sets the typewriter rate; 30 reads slowly, 60 quickly, 1000 is effectively instant. The first advance press completes the line, the second dismisses it, so an impatient player double\-taps. A "skip all" is the scene setting \fBconvo = NULL\fR and \fBdlg.active = false\fR on a key.
.PP
.SH SOUND
Play a short blip from update every few characters: keep \fBlast_shown\fR in a static, and when \fB(int)dlg.chars_shown / 3\fR changes, \fBaudio_play(&blip, 0.5f)\fR.
.PP
.SH PITFALLS
.IP \(bu 2
Calling dialog_update from both handle_input and update: the typing runs twice as fast and presses count twice. One or the other.
.IP \(bu 2
Passing a local buffer: the text is dangling by the next frame.
.IP \(bu 2
Lines longer than three wrapped lines: cut off. Split them into two dialog_show calls.
.IP \(bu 2
Forgetting to call dialog_render last: the box sits under the HUD.
.IP \(bu 2
Reading the advance keys yourself while a dialog is active: both fire.
